## Problem

When a test class uses `@Nested` inner classes to group tests, a `@StartStop MockWebServer` field declared on the outer class is never started. Any test inside a nested class that touches the server (e.g. `mockWebServer.url(...)`) fails with:

```
java.lang.IllegalStateException: call start() first
    at mockwebserver3.MockWebServer.getSocketAddress(MockWebServer.kt:150)
    at mockwebserver3.MockWebServer.getHostName(MockWebServer.kt:156)
    at mockwebserver3.MockWebServer.url(MockWebServer.kt:200)
```

## Minimal reproduction

```kotlin
class NestedStartStopTest {
    @StartStop
    private val mockWebServer = MockWebServer()

    @Nested
    inner class Group {
        @Test
        fun `server is running`() {
            // fails: IllegalStateException: call start() first
            mockWebServer.url("/")
        }
    }
}
```

Running the same assertion in a top-level `@Test` on the outer class works as expected.

Environment: `com.squareup.okhttp3:mockwebserver3-junit5:5.3.2`, JUnit Jupiter 5.x.

## Root cause

In [`StartStopExtension.beforeEach`](https://github.com/square/okhttp/blob/127cfe25168931aa600fff29c43f96d8d1dd480c/mockwebserver-junit5/src/main/kotlin/mockwebserver3/junit5/internal/StartStopExtension.kt#L50-L67):

```kotlin
override fun beforeEach(context: ExtensionContext) {
  val testInstance = context.testInstance.get()
  val instanceFields =
    findAnnotatedFields(
      context.requiredTestClass,
      StartStop::class.java,
    ) { !Modifier.isStatic(it.modifiers) }
  for (field in instanceFields) {
    ...
    val server = field.get(testInstance) as? MockWebServer ?: continue
    ...
    server.start()
  }
}
```

For a nested test:
- `context.testInstance.get()` returns the **inner-class** instance, not the outer one that holds the field.
- `context.requiredTestClass` is the **inner** `@Nested` class, so `findAnnotatedFields` never sees fields declared on enclosing classes.

Result: the outer `@StartStop` field is invisible to the extension, `server.start()` is never invoked, and any nested test that uses the server hits `IllegalStateException`.

## Suggested fix

Iterate `context.requiredTestInstances.allInstances` (which contains outer instances in order) and, for each, enumerate `@StartStop`-annotated fields on that instance's class:

```kotlin
override fun beforeEach(context: ExtensionContext) {
  val store = context.getStore(Namespace.create(StartStop::class.java))
  for (testInstance in context.requiredTestInstances.allInstances) {
    val fields = findAnnotatedFields(
      testInstance.javaClass,
      StartStop::class.java,
    ) { !Modifier.isStatic(it.modifiers) }
    for (field in fields) {
      field.setAccessible(true)
      val server = field.get(testInstance) as? MockWebServer ?: continue
      store.put(field, server)
      server.start()
    }
  }
}
```

This matches the pattern JUnit 5 recommends for extensions that need to work with `@Nested`.

## Current workarounds

- Manual `@BeforeEach`/`@AfterEach` on the outer class calling `mockWebServer.start()`/`.close()`. Outer-class `@BeforeEach` runs before each nested test.
- `@TestInstance(Lifecycle.PER_CLASS)` + `@BeforeAll`/`@AfterAll` — server shared across all nested tests.
- Duplicate the `@StartStop` field on every `@Nested` class.
- Drop `@Nested` and flatten the test class.

### Description

With a `basic_json` whose `number_float_t` is `long double` (where `long double` is not binary64, i.e. x86-64 and aarch64 Linux), `dump()` writes corrupt output under a locale whose decimal point is a multibyte sequence. glibc's `ps_AF` locale has decimal_point U+066B (bytes `D9 AB`) and thousands_sep U+066C (bytes `D9 AC`). `dump()` of `1.5L` returns the bytes `1 \xab 5 . 0`, which is not JSON and does not parse back.

The default `json` (double) and `float` configurations are not affected: they take the `to_chars` path. The lexer handles the same locale correctly since #5660 (`detail::convert_float_locale_aware()` treats the decimal point as a string), so values parse but do not dump.

### Reproduction steps

Linux with glibc (gcc:16 Docker image, glibc 2.41):

```sh
apt-get install -y locales && localedef -i ps_AF -f UTF-8 ps_AF.UTF-8
g++ -std=c++17 -I include repro.cpp -o repro && ./repro
```

### Expected vs. actual results

Expected: `1.5` (dump is documented as locale-independent, `docs/mkdocs/docs/features/types/number_handling.md`).

Actual:

```
dump: 1\xab5.0   (expected 1.5)
does not parse back: [json.exception.parse_error.101] parse error at line 1, column 2: syntax error while parsing value - invalid literal; last read: '1\xd9'; expected end of input
```

More values under `ps_AF.UTF-8`: `123456.789L` → `123456\xab788999999999999999999999999999.0`, `1e100L` → `9\xab99999999999999999999999999999999963e+99`, `1.5e-7L` → `1\xab50000000000000000000000000000000008e-07`; `2.0L` → `2.0` (no fraction, correct). Under `C`, `de_DE.UTF-8` and `fa_IR.UTF-8` (decimal point `.` in glibc) everything is correct.

### Minimal code example

```cpp
#include <clocale>
#include <cstdint>
#include <cstdio>
#include <map>
#include <vector>
#include <nlohmann/json.hpp>
using ldjson = nlohmann::basic_json<std::map, std::vector, std::string, bool, std::int64_t, std::uint64_t, long double>;
int main()
{
    if (std::setlocale(LC_ALL, "ps_AF.UTF-8") == nullptr) { return 2; }
    const ldjson j = 1.5L;
    const std::string s = j.dump();
    for (const unsigned char c : s) { std::printf(c < 0x80 ? "%c" : "\\x%02x", c); }
    std::printf("\n");
    try { (void)ldjson::parse(s); }
    catch (const ldjson::exception& e) { std::printf("does not parse back: %s\n", e.what()); return 1; }
    return s == "1.5" ? 0 : 1;
}
```

### Error messages

See above (parse_error.101 when reading the dumped text back).

### Compiler and operating system

gcc 16.2 / glibc 2.41 (Debian trixie, `gcc:16` image), aarch64 and x86-64. Not reproducible where `long double` is binary64 (Apple Silicon, MSVC), because those take the `to_chars` path.

### Library version

develop at ddc34f546a; the same code is in 3.12.0 (`serializer` stored `*loc->thousands_sep` / `*loc->decimal_point` as `char` at construction) and earlier.

### Validation

- [x] The bug also occurs if the latest version from the [`develop`](https://github.com/nlohmann/json/tree/develop) branch is used.
- [x] I can successfully [compile and run the unit tests](https://github.com/nlohmann/json#execute-unit-tests).

### Root cause

[`include/nlohmann/detail/output/serializer.hpp`, `dump_float(number_float_t, std::false_type)`](https://github.com/nlohmann/json/blob/ddc34f546a/include/nlohmann/detail/output/serializer.hpp#L2085-L2137), the `snprintf` path used when `number_float_t` is not IEEE single or double:

- lines 2102-2103 read `std::localeconv()->thousands_sep` and `->decimal_point` as single `char`s, i.e. their first byte only (`0xD9` for both in `ps_AF`);
- lines 2106-2112 `std::remove()` that byte from the buffer, which strips the first byte of the decimal point (`%.*Lg` never emits grouping, so this step has nothing legitimate to remove);
- lines 2115-2123 then search for the decimal point's first byte, find nothing, and the stray `0xAB` stays;
- lines 2127-2137 append `.0` when neither `.` nor `e` is found.

Even for a multibyte decimal point whose first byte differs from the thousands separator, only the first byte would be replaced by `.` and the rest would remain (`1.\xab5`).

### Suggested fix

Treat both as byte strings, as `convert_float_locale_aware()` in `number_parse.hpp` already does for parsing: take `std::strlen(loc->decimal_point)`, find the full sequence with `std::search` and replace it with `.`; drop the thousands-separator removal (dead for `%g`), or make it string-based too. Alternatively format `long double` with `std::to_chars` where the standard library provides it and keep `snprintf` as the fallback. A regression test can sit next to the multibyte parse test in `tests/src/unit-locale-cpp.cpp` (lines 354-403), which already relies on a generated `ps_AF`/`fa_IR` locale on Linux.

Related: #5660 fixed the parse side of the same family (lexer under `fa_IR.UTF-8`); #5709/#5729 moved the locale lookup to dump time but kept the single-byte reads.

